@doc """
Extractor module: Pattern-based validation and decomposition.

A named `defextractor` or builtin Extractor is used as a Pattern head.
Its last declared input receives the value being matched. Earlier inputs are
ordinary expressions supplied before the output child Patterns. The body returns
`MatchResult::Ok(payload)` on success or `MatchResult::Err(error)` on failure.
Named Extractors are declarations in modules or impls; they are not ordinary
callable values. The auto-imported `uncons` is a ready-to-use named Extractor.

The matched input must have a concrete static type head. A bare generic input
such as `value: $T`, or a Trait constraint intended to accept unrelated types,
is rejected. Generic arguments under a concrete head remain valid, for example
`value: List<$T>`. Put type-wide generic or Trait-constrained computation in a
normal function.

## Named Extractors
Declare a named Extractor inside a module or impl in a source file:

```surtr
defmod Checks {
  defextractor positive(value: Int) -> MatchResult<Unit, Error> {
    True =? value > 0
    MatchResult::Ok(())
  }
}
```

After loading that module, `is_match(2, Checks::positive())` is True.
The following examples use the standard `uncons` and need no extra module:

xldr(1)> match [1, 2, 3] { uncons(head, tail) => (head, tail), _ => (0, []) }
(1, [2, 3])
xldr(2)> is_match("surtr", uncons("s", _))
True

## ExtractorClosure and pre-arguments
`*{|inputs...| body}` creates an ExtractorClosure. It captures outer values and
can be passed to helpers, but its application is still a Pattern. Bind it to a
variable before using it as a head. Ordinary calls such as `shift(3)` and direct
literal heads are not supported.

Binding an ExtractorClosure to a variable requires a concrete signature. Add a
binding or parameter annotation when the literal does not determine every input
and payload type. When a literal is passed directly to a higher-order function,
the function's expected ExtractorClosure type may determine those types without
extra annotations. Normal closures and captures use the same callable boundary.

xldr(1)> base = 10
xldr(2)> shift = *{|amount: Int, value: Int| MatchResult::Ok(value + amount + base)}
xldr(3)> apply_pattern(3, shift(2, _1: Int))
Ok(15)
xldr(4)> match 3 { shift(2, result) => result, _ => 0 }
15

The matched value and each reached Extractor are evaluated once. Pre-arguments
are evaluated left to right when that head is reached. Pre-arguments, pins and
heads resolve in the scope outside the whole Pattern, so a preceding child
binding does not change the meaning of a later pre-argument or `^name`.

Extractor bodies do not restrict effects or computational cost. Process
messaging and IO handlers remain available through their typed interfaces. The
language guarantees the static types crossing feature boundaries and immutable
values, not purity, execution cost, or zero-cost abstraction.

## Unit payloads and failure
A Unit success payload permits no child Pattern or one explicit child. The
explicit child can be a binding, wildcard, or projection, with a Unit annotation.

xldr(1)> positive = *{|value: Int| True =? value > 0; MatchResult::Ok(())}
xldr(2)> is_match(2, positive())
True
xldr(3)> is_match(0, positive(_: Unit))
False
xldr(4)> apply_pattern(2, positive(_1: Unit))
Ok(())

`match`, `if_let`, `if_let_then`, and `is_match` treat an Extractor error as a
failed branch. `apply_pattern` returns that same Error in `Err`, including its
kind, message, location and cause. An ordinary structural mismatch also returns
`Err`. OR Patterns belong to the branch consumers, not `apply_pattern`.

## Projection results
`apply_pattern(value, Pattern)` returns `Result` and makes selected values
available without exposing Pattern bindings to the surrounding scope. No slots
produce `Ok(())`; one slot produces its value; multiple slots produce a tuple in
numeric order. Slots `_1` through `_16` must be unique and contiguous. They may
carry annotations or select an alias or list tail. `_`, `_name` and `_foo1` remain
wildcards. Numbered slots are not ordinary variables.

xldr(1)> apply_pattern(3, 3)
Ok(())
xldr(2)> apply_pattern(("tag", 3), (_2: String, _1: Int))
Ok((3, "tag"))
xldr(3)> apply_pattern([1, 2, 3], [_1: Int, .._2: List<Int>])
Ok((1, [2, 3]))
xldr(4)> apply_pattern([1, 2], [_, _] @ _1: List<Int>)
Ok([1, 2])
xldr(5)> [1, 2] |> apply_pattern(uncons(_1, _2))
Ok((1, [2]))
xldr(6)> apply_pattern(Ok(3), _1)
Ok(Ok(3))
xldr(7)> apply_pattern(Ok(3), Ok(_1))
Ok(3)

The input is matched whole; `apply_pattern` does not unwrap an input Result.
In a pipe, only an outer direct Expr argument can receive the input; a projection
in the Pattern is always an output slot.

## SafeBind
Within a Result-returning callable, `=?` preserves the failing Error and stops
the callable. In an Extractor body it instead returns `MatchResult::Err` with the
same Error. A nested callable or `do` expression keeps its own failure target.

xldr(1)> positive = *{|value: Int| True =? value > 0; MatchResult::Ok(())}
xldr(2)> checked: (Int -> Result<Int>) = {|value: Int| positive() =? Ok(value); Ok(value)}
xldr(3)> checked(3)
Ok(3)
xldr(4)> is_match(checked(0), Err(_))
True

## Adapting Result callables
`Extractor::from_result` adapts a single-input Result-returning callable explicitly.
The returned ExtractorClosure retains the success payload shape and the original
Error. See `:doc Extractor::from_result` for scalar, tuple and Unit examples.

xldr(1)> decimal = Extractor::from_result(&Int::parse)
xldr(2)> apply_pattern("42", decimal(_1: Int))
Ok(42)
xldr(3)> is_match(apply_pattern("oops", decimal(_1)), Err(_))
True
"""
defmod Extractor {
  @doc """
  Adapt one Result-returning callable to an ExtractorClosure.

  `f: ($A -> Result<$B>)` becomes
  `ExtractorClosure<($A -> MatchResult<$B>)>`. Creating the value captures
  `f` without calling it. Each reached Pattern occurrence calls it exactly once.
  `Ok(payload)` preserves the payload; `Err(error)` preserves the original Error
  and cause chain. Only the outer Result is unwrapped, even when its payload is
  another Result.

  Bind the returned value before using it as a Pattern head. To adapt a function
  with several inputs, pass a single-input ordinary Closure that explicitly
  captures the other values. Option-returning or raw-value callables are not
  accepted, and no implicit callable conversion is performed.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> decimal = Extractor::from_result(&Int::parse)
  xldr(2)> apply_pattern("42", decimal(_1: Int))
  Ok(42)
  xldr(3)> is_match(apply_pattern("oops", decimal(_1)), Err(_))
  True
  xldr(4)> pair = Extractor::from_result({|value: Int| Ok((value, value + 1))})
  xldr(5)> apply_pattern(3, pair(_1, _2))
  Ok((3, 4))
  xldr(6)> done = Extractor::from_result({|value: Int| Ok(())})
  xldr(7)> apply_pattern(3, done())
  Ok(())
  xldr(8)> apply_pattern(3, done(_1: Unit))
  Ok(())
  xldr(9)> nested = Extractor::from_result({|value: Int| Ok(Ok(value))})
  xldr(10)> apply_pattern(3, nested(_1))
  Ok(Ok(3))
  xldr(11)> amount = 10
  xldr(12)> shifted = Extractor::from_result({|value: Int| Ok(value + amount)})
  xldr(13)> apply_pattern(3, shifted(_1))
  Ok(13)
  """
  def from_result(f: ($A -> Result<$B>)) -> ExtractorClosure<($A -> MatchResult<$B>)> {
    *{|value: $A|
      converted =? f(value)
      MatchResult::Ok(converted)
    }
  }
}
